WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.709
Here's that bung apparatus.

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:04.440
The bung goes round in a horizontal circle,

00:00:04.883 --> 00:00:07.031
and the weight of the hanging masses keeps

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:07.894
the string tight.

00:00:08.757 --> 00:00:10.722
Suppose we keep the string length the same,

00:00:11.124 --> 00:00:12.768
and hang four times as much mass.

00:00:13.551 --> 00:00:15.515
How many times faster will the bung have

00:00:15.536 --> 00:00:17.501
to turn, in revolutions per second?

00:00:18.528 --> 00:00:19.112
Make a prediction.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.173
First, how does it work?

00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.359
The hanging masses sit still,

00:00:26.861 --> 00:00:29.365
so the string's tension equals their weight, M

00:00:29.446 --> 00:00:29.666
g.

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:32.335
You set that tension just by choosing the

00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:32.656
masses.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.904
The paper clip on the string is a

00:00:35.066 --> 00:00:35.466
marker.

00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:38.152
If it stays just below the tube, the

00:00:38.194 --> 00:00:40.056
length of string out to the bung, L,

00:00:40.338 --> 00:00:40.938
stays the same.

00:00:41.861 --> 00:00:43.624
In practice, the tube has to be moved

00:00:43.705 --> 00:00:45.508
in a tiny circle to keep the bung

00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:45.829
going.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:48.359
Your school will have its own approved method

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.349
and safety steps, so follow those.

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:52.328
Look closely, though.

00:00:53.076 --> 00:00:55.163
The bung's weight makes the string droop a

00:00:55.365 --> 00:00:58.295
little below horizontal, just like the conical pendulum.

00:00:58.718 --> 00:01:01.065
So only the horizontal part of the tension

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:02.269
pulls the bung inwards,

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:04.698
and the circle's radius is a bit shorter

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:04.899
than L.

00:01:05.964 --> 00:01:08.214
Both are shrunk by the same factor, the

00:01:08.275 --> 00:01:09.842
cosine of the droop angle.

00:01:10.747 --> 00:01:13.034
Now write the inward force as m v

00:01:13.116 --> 00:01:15.704
squared over r, which is m omega squared

00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:15.865
r.

00:01:16.669 --> 00:01:18.796
The same factor appears on both sides and

00:01:18.897 --> 00:01:22.449
cancels, leaving M g equals m omega squared

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:22.509
L.

00:01:23.397 --> 00:01:24.468
That's the form we can test.

00:01:25.116 --> 00:01:25.982
Let's set up the test.

00:01:26.545 --> 00:01:29.127
The independent variable, the one we change, is

00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:31.129
the hanging mass, which sets the tension.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.433
The dependent variable, the one we measure, is

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:37.175
how fast the bung turns, from its period.

00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:40.123
And the controlled variables, the ones we keep

00:01:40.145 --> 00:01:42.130
the same, are the string length and the

00:01:42.171 --> 00:01:42.752
bung's mass.

00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:46.128
We can't read omega directly, so we measure

00:01:46.168 --> 00:01:47.652
a time and calculate it.

00:01:48.396 --> 00:01:51.349
Time twenty revolutions with a stopwatch, and divide

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:52.755
by twenty to get the period.

00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.560
Timing twenty, not one, spreads your reaction time

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.141
over many turns, so the period is much

00:01:59.201 --> 00:01:59.822
more precise.

00:02:00.485 --> 00:02:02.872
Repeat each run three times, and average.

00:02:03.735 --> 00:02:06.356
Then omega is one full turn, two pi

00:02:06.397 --> 00:02:09.679
radians, divided by the period, so omega equals

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.740
two pi over T.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.231
Now the prediction.

00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:16.497
If M g equals m omega squared L,

00:02:17.078 --> 00:02:18.678
with m and L fixed,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.720
then the hanging weight is proportional to omega

00:02:21.781 --> 00:02:22.261
squared.

00:02:22.962 --> 00:02:26.370
So plot M g against omega squared, not

00:02:26.452 --> 00:02:27.233
against omega.

00:02:27.996 --> 00:02:29.979
The prediction is a straight line through the

00:02:30.041 --> 00:02:32.646
origin, with a gradient of m times L.

00:02:33.649 --> 00:02:36.050
Four times the hanging weight means four times

00:02:36.171 --> 00:02:36.991
omega squared,

00:02:37.552 --> 00:02:39.513
so the bung turns twice as fast.

00:02:40.342 --> 00:02:41.189
Here's a set of results.

00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:43.903
These are illustrative values, set up to show

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:45.631
how you'd process real class data.

00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:47.714
With a hundred grams hanging,

00:02:48.255 --> 00:02:50.797
M g is zero point one zero zero

00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:53.358
times nine point eight, which is zero point

00:02:53.438 --> 00:02:54.478
nine eight newtons.

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:57.809
Twenty revolutions took eighteen point one seconds, so

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.921
T is zero point nine zero five seconds.

00:03:01.504 --> 00:03:04.488
Then omega is two pi over zero point

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:07.133
nine zero five, about six point nine four

00:03:07.194 --> 00:03:10.078
radians per second, and omega squared is about

00:03:10.139 --> 00:03:10.999
forty-eight point two.

00:03:11.782 --> 00:03:13.537
Do that for each row, and plot the

00:03:13.577 --> 00:03:13.819
points.

00:03:14.703 --> 00:03:16.910
In these illustrative results they lie close to

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.474
a straight line through the origin.

00:03:19.197 --> 00:03:21.588
The line's gradient is about zero point zero

00:03:21.628 --> 00:03:23.095
two zero kilogram metres.

00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:25.623
Dividing by the string length should give the

00:03:25.663 --> 00:03:28.229
bung's mass, and it gives about zero point

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:29.692
zero two five four kilograms.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.385
The balance said zero point zero two five,

00:03:32.768 --> 00:03:34.356
so they agree to about two percent.

00:03:35.121 --> 00:03:37.333
Real measurements are the real test, but this

00:03:37.394 --> 00:03:38.218
is how you'd process them.

00:03:38.868 --> 00:03:41.299
Each timing is uncertain by about plus or

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.549
minus a fifth of a second, from reaction

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:43.831
time.

00:03:44.495 --> 00:03:47.128
Over eighteen seconds, that's about one percent in

00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:47.651
the period.

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:51.604
Omega squared squares that, so its uncertainty doubles,

00:03:51.905 --> 00:03:52.947
to around two percent.

00:03:53.751 --> 00:03:55.561
Each point gets an error bar, and the

00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:57.131
prediction line passes through them all.

00:03:57.694 --> 00:03:59.740
So these numbers are consistent with the model,

00:03:59.982 --> 00:04:00.985
within the uncertainty.

00:04:01.728 --> 00:04:04.838
Repeating runs and averaging reduces the effect of

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.808
random errors, like reaction time, on the mean.

00:04:08.451 --> 00:04:11.017
It can't fix a systematic error, one that

00:04:11.058 --> 00:04:12.801
pushes every reading the same way.

00:04:13.544 --> 00:04:15.572
Friction where the string rubs on the tube

00:04:15.673 --> 00:04:16.013
is one.

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:19.161
A marker that slowly drifts is another, because

00:04:19.203 --> 00:04:21.748
then the string length isn't really eighty centimetres.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:24.515
You control those with the method, like a

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:26.881
smooth tube and a steady marker, not by

00:04:26.942 --> 00:04:27.322
repeating.

00:04:28.026 --> 00:04:30.535
And follow your school's safety steps, such as

00:04:30.717 --> 00:04:32.784
safety glasses, because a bung can fly off.

00:04:33.448 --> 00:04:35.812
The same apparatus can test the other variables

00:04:35.874 --> 00:04:36.093
too:

00:04:36.696 --> 00:04:38.900
change the string length, or the bung's mass,

00:04:39.362 --> 00:04:40.905
and keep everything else fixed.

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.157
A common mistake is plotting the hanging weight

00:04:44.358 --> 00:04:45.178
against omega.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.926
That gives a curve, and it's easy to

00:04:48.088 --> 00:04:49.893
wrongly decide the model has failed.

00:04:50.737 --> 00:04:51.965
Here's a way to remember the fix.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.752
Square the speed to straighten the line.

00:04:57.417 --> 00:04:57.820
Your turn.

00:04:58.486 --> 00:05:00.989
In a whirling bung experiment, there's zero point

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.433
five zero metres of string between the tube

00:05:03.534 --> 00:05:04.034
and the bung,

00:05:04.576 --> 00:05:06.799
the bung's mass is twenty grams, and a

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.501
hundred grams hangs from the string.

00:05:09.145 --> 00:05:12.040
The bung makes twenty revolutions in fourteen point

00:05:12.082 --> 00:05:12.805
zero seconds.

00:05:13.488 --> 00:05:15.552
Calculate m omega squared L,

00:05:16.174 --> 00:05:18.177
compare it with the hanging weight, and suggest

00:05:18.439 --> 00:05:19.641
one reason for any difference.

00:05:20.345 --> 00:05:21.230
Pause here to answer.

00:05:31.907 --> 00:05:35.011
The period is fourteen over twenty, zero point

00:05:35.052 --> 00:05:35.992
seven zero seconds,

00:05:36.554 --> 00:05:39.297
so omega is two pi over zero point

00:05:39.318 --> 00:05:42.241
seven zero, about eight point nine eight radians

00:05:42.282 --> 00:05:42.762
per second.

00:05:43.464 --> 00:05:43.703
Then

00:05:44.205 --> 00:05:46.885
m omega squared L is about zero point

00:05:46.987 --> 00:05:47.986
eight one newtons,

00:05:48.588 --> 00:05:50.668
but the hanging weight is zero point nine

00:05:50.789 --> 00:05:51.369
eight newtons.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.374
One reason is friction between the string and

00:05:54.395 --> 00:05:56.439
the tube, so less than the full weight

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:57.301
reaches the bung.

00:05:58.065 --> 00:06:00.541
The classic slip is using fourteen seconds as

00:06:00.582 --> 00:06:01.064
the period.

00:06:02.649 --> 00:06:03.051
Last one.

00:06:04.038 --> 00:06:06.920
A student plots the hanging weight against omega

00:06:06.941 --> 00:06:09.803
squared, for a bung on zero point four

00:06:09.844 --> 00:06:11.124
zero metres of string.

00:06:12.087 --> 00:06:13.970
The graph is a straight line through the

00:06:14.071 --> 00:06:16.856
origin, with a gradient of zero point zero

00:06:16.898 --> 00:06:19.061
zero eight zero kilogram metres.

00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:21.372
Determine the mass of the bung.

00:06:22.439 --> 00:06:23.283
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.994 --> 00:06:37.419
M g equals m omega squared L, so

00:06:37.621 --> 00:06:40.765
the gradient of M g against omega squared

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.468
is m times L.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.471
So m is the gradient divided by L,

00:06:45.892 --> 00:06:48.633
zero point zero zero eight zero over zero

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:51.376
point four zero, which is zero point zero

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:53.918
two zero kilograms, or twenty grams.

00:06:54.719 --> 00:06:57.585
The slip is multiplying by L instead, and

00:06:57.626 --> 00:07:00.492
getting zero point zero zero three two kilograms.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.681
So the bung moves in a circle because

00:07:03.701 --> 00:07:05.263
the string pulls it inwards,

00:07:05.863 --> 00:07:08.065
and measurements like these are how we test

00:07:08.345 --> 00:07:10.346
F equals m v squared over r.

00:07:11.308 --> 00:07:12.236
Now think about the Moon.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:14.205
It goes round the Earth about once a

00:07:14.265 --> 00:07:15.891
month, with no string at all.

00:07:16.473 --> 00:07:17.937
Something must still pull it inwards,

00:07:18.439 --> 00:07:19.362
and that's gravity.

00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:22.130
So how does the pull of gravity depend

00:07:22.191 --> 00:07:24.656
on the masses involved, and on the distance

00:07:24.717 --> 00:07:25.559
from the Earth's centre?

00:07:28.428 --> 00:07:29.250
Here are the key notes.

00:07:29.774 --> 00:07:31.321
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:31.362 --> 00:07:31.783
to copy them.
